\label{thm:holdingtimes_distn}
Assume \eqref{eq:mainthmcondition2} holds.
Then, as $N\to\infty$, the finite-dimensional distributions of $\varpi_1^{(N)} , \varpi_2^{(N)} , \dots$ converge to those of $\varpi_1, \varpi_2, \dots$, where the $\varpi_i$ are independent $\operatorname{Exp}(\alpha_n)$-distributed random variables.

For any $k \in \mathbb{N}$, there is a continuous bijection between the jump times $T_{ 1 : k }^{(N)}$ and the holding times $\varpi_{ 1 : k }^{(N)}$.
Thus, convergence of $\varpi_{ 1 : k }^{(N)}$ to $\varpi_{ 1 : k }$ is equivalent to convergence of the jump times to $T_{ 1 : k }$, where $T_i := \varpi_1+\dots+\varpi_i$. 
We will work with the jump times, following \citet[Lemma 3.2]{mohle1999}.
The idea is to prove by induction that, for any $k\in\mathbb{N}$ and $t_{ 1 : k } > 0$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:518}
\lim_{N\to\infty} \mathbb{P}\left[ T_{ 1 : k }^{(N)} \leq t_{ 1 : k } \right]
= \mathbb{P}[ T_{ 1 : k } \leq t_{ 1 : k } ] .
\end{equation}
Take the basis case $k=1$, for which $\mathbb{P}[ T_1 \leq t ] 
= \mathbb{P}[ \varpi_1 \leq t ] = 1 - e^{-\alpha_n t}$
and $T_1^{(N)} >t$ if and only if $( Z_{ \tau_N( t ) } )$ has no jumps up to time $t$:
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{P}\left[T_1^{(N)} >t \right]
= \mathbb{E}\left[ \mathbb{P}[ T_1^{(N)} >t \mid \mathcal{F}_\infty ] \right]
= \mathbb{E}\Bigg[ \prod_{r=1}^{\tau_N(t)} (1-p_r) \Bigg] .
\end{equation*}
Lemma~\ref{thm:basis} shows that this probability converges to $e^{-\alpha_n t}$ as required.

For the induction step, assume that \eqref{eq:518} holds for some $k$. 
We have
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}\left[ T_{ 1 : ( k + 1 ) }^{(N)} \leq t_{ 1 : ( k + 1 ) } \right]
= \mathbb{P}\left[ T_{ 1 : k }^{(N)} \leq t_{ 1 : k } \right] - \mathbb{P}\left[ T_{ 1 :  k }^{(N)} \leq t_{ 1 : k }, T_{k+1}^{(N)} > t_{k+1} \right].
\end{align*}
The first term on the right-hand side converges to $\mathbb{P}[ T_{ 1 : k } \leq t_{ 1 : k } ]$ by the induction hypothesis, and it remains to show that
\begin{align}
\lim_{N\to\infty} 
        &\mathbb{P}\left[ T_{ 1 : k }^{(N)} \leq t_{ 1 : k }, T_{k+1}^{(N)} > t_{k+1} \right] = \mathbb{P}[ T_{ 1 : k }\leq t_{ 1 : k }, T_{k+1} > t_{k+1} ]. \label{eq:induction}
\end{align}
As shown in \citet[p.\ 459]{mohle1999},
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{P}[ T_{ 1 : k }\leq t_{ 1 : k }, T_{k+1} > t_{k+1} ]
= \alpha_n^k e^{-\alpha_n t} 
        \sum_{\substack{i_1\leq \dots\leq i_{k-1}\\ \in \{0,\dots,k\} :
        \\ i_j \geq j \forall j}} 
        \prod_{j=1}^k \frac{(t_j - t_{j-1})^{i_j - i_{j-1}}}{(i_j - i_{j-1})! } ,
\end{equation*}
while the probability on the left-hand side of \eqref{eq:induction} can be written
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}\left[ T_{ 1 : k }^{(N)} \leq t_{ 1 : k }, T_{k+1}^{(N)} > t_{k+1} \right] &= \mathbb{E}\left[ \mathbb{P}[ T_{ 1 : k }^{(N)} \leq t_{ 1 : k }, T_{k+1}^{(N)} > t_{k+1} 
        \mid \mathcal{F}_\infty] \right] \\
&= \mathbb{E} \Bigg[ \sum_{\substack{r_1 <\dots< r_k :
        \\ r_i \leq \tau_N(t_i) \forall i}}
        \Big( \prod_{i=1}^k p_{r_i} \Big)
        \Bigg( \prod_{\substack{r=1 \\ \notin \{r_1,\dots,r_k\} }}^{\tau_N(t)} 
        (1-p_r) \Bigg) \Bigg] .
\end{align*}
That is, there are jumps at some times $r_{ 1 : k }$, and identity transitions at all other times.
A similar expression is derived in \citet{mohle1999}, but here we have an additional expectation because the probabilities $p_r$ depend on the random offspring counts.
Lemmata \ref{thm:inductionUB} and \ref{thm:inductionLB} show that this probability converges to the correct limit.
This completes the induction.

